.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OCSP_SENDREQ_NEW 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm OCSP_REQ_CTX ,
.Nm OCSP_sendreq_new ,
.Nm OCSP_sendreq_nbio ,
.Nm OCSP_sendreq_bio ,
.Nm OCSP_REQ_CTX_i2d ,
.Nm OCSP_REQ_CTX_add1_header ,
.Nm OCSP_REQ_CTX_free ,
.Nm OCSP_set_max_response_length ,
.Nm OCSP_REQ_CTX_set1_req
.Nd OCSP responder query functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ocsp.h
.Ft OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX *
.Fo OCSP_sendreq_new
.Fa "BIO *io"
.Fa "const char *path"
.Fa "const OCSP_REQUEST *req"
.Fa "int buf_size"
.Fc
.Ft OCSP_RESPONSE *
.Fo OCSP_sendreq_bio
.Fa "BIO *io"
.Fa "const char *path"
.Fa "OCSP_REQUEST *req"
.Fc
The following functions have been deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0, and can
be hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft typedef OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX OCSP_REQ_CTX;
 int
.Fo OCSP_sendreq_nbio
.Fa "OCSP_RESPONSE **presp"
.Fa "OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX *rctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OCSP_REQ_CTX_i2d
.Fa "OCSP_REQ_CT *rctx"
.Fa "const ASN1_ITEM *it"
.Fa "ASN1_VALUE *req"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OCSP_REQ_CTX_add1_header
.Fa "OCSP_REQ_CT *rctx"
.Fa "const char *name"
.Fa "const char *value"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OCSP_REQ_CTX_free
.Fa "OCSP_REQ_CTX *rctx"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OCSP_set_max_response_length
.Fa "OCSP_REQ_CT *rctx"
.Fa "unsigned long len"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OCSP_REQ_CTX_set1_req
.Fa "OCSP_REQ_CTX *rctx"
.Fa "const OCSP_REQUEST *req"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions perform an OCSP POST request / response transfer over
HTTP, using the HTTP request functions described in
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX 3 .
.Pp
The function
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_new
builds a complete
.Vt OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX
structure with the
.Vt BIO
.Fa io
to be used for requests and response, the URL path
.Fa path ,
optionally the OCSP request
.Fa req ,
and a response header maximum line length of
.Fa buf_size .
If
.Fa buf_size
is zero a default value of 4KiB is used.
The
.Fa req
may be set to NULL and provided later using
.Fn OCSP_REQ_CTX_set1_req
or
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req 3 .
The
.Fa io
and
.Fa path
arguments to
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_new
correspond to the components of the URL. For example if the responder
URL is
.Qo Li http://example.com/ocspreq Qc
the BIO
.Fa io
should haven been connected to host
.Qo Li example.com Qc
on port 80 and
.Fa path
should be set to
.Qo Li /ocspreq Qc .
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_nbio
attempts to send the request prepared in
.Fa rctx
and to gather the response via HTTP, using the BIO
.Fa io
and
.Fa path
that were given when calling
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_new .
If the operation gets completed it assigns the response, a pointer to a
.Vt OCSP_RESPONSE
structure, in
.Em *presp .
The function may need to be called again if its result is -1, which
indicates
.Xr BIO_should_retry 3 .
In such a case it is advisable to sleep a little in between, using
.Xr BIO_wait 3
on the read BIO to prevent a busy loop.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_bio
combines
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_new
with as many calls of
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_nbio
as needed and then
.Fn OCSP_REQ_CTX_free ,
with a response header maximum line length 4k.
It waits indefinitely on a response.
It does not support setting a timeout or adding headers and is retained
for compatibility; use
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_transfer 3
instead.
.Pp
OCSP_REQ_CTX_i2d(rctx, it, req) is equivalent to the following:
.Bd -literal
  OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req(rctx, "application/ocsp-request", it, req)
.Ed
.Pp
OCSP_REQ_CTX_set1_req(rctx, req) is equivalent to the following:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req(rctx, "application/ocsp-request",
                            ASN1_ITEM_rptr(OCSP_REQUEST),
                            (const ASN1_VALUE *)req)
.Ed
.Pp
The deprecated type and the remaining deprecated functions have been
superseded by the following equivalents:
.Vt OCSP_REQ_CTX
by
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX 3 ,
.Fn OCSP_REQ_CTX_add1_header
by
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_add1_header 3 ,
.Fn OCSP_REQ_CTX_free
by
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_free 3 ,
and
.Fn OCSP_set_max_response_length
by
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_max_response_length 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_new
returns a valid
.Vt OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX
structure or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_nbio
returns 1 for success, 0 on error, -1 if retry is needed.
.Pp
.Fn OCSP_sendreq_bio
returns the
.Vt OCSP_RESPONSE
structure sent by the responder or NULL if an error occurred.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX 3 ,
